## Web UI fallback

If you're stuck on a config.xml surgery problem that takes more than 20 minutes, **ssh-tunnel the web UI on port 443 and configure through it**. OPNsense is built for the web UI; scripted automation is tolerated but not supported-first. The web UI's "Save" button triggers the exact same apply pipeline (`configctl ... reload`), so once it works there you can round-trip the config.xml to see what the UI generated.

## Verify before concluding anything

After any non-trivial config:

```
configctl firewall list rules | head -30      # what pf actually thinks the ruleset is
pfctl -s nat                                  # what NAT is active
ifconfig                                      # interface addresses
ip -4 addr 2>/dev/null || ifconfig             # FreeBSD uses ifconfig, not ip
netstat -rn                                   # routing table
tcpdump -i <iface> -nn -c 10 host <peer>      # what's on the wire
```

FreeBSD uses `ifconfig`, `netstat -rn`, `pfctl` — not Linux's `ip` / `ss`. Don't try to `ip route show` — it won't exist.

